    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • OMODA 4 Lands in Paris To Showcase Futuristic Styling, Looks Like a Discount Urus\nModified F82 BMW M4 Is Now a Track-Ready Missile, Lowered, Enhanced, and Ready for Body Hugging\n9 photos\nPhoto: Garage Kept Motors / Instagram\nSitting proudly in the inventory of Grand Rapids, Michigan-based Garage Kept Motors , this 2015 BMW M4 has the 3.0L Twin Turbo inline-six ready to fight any opponent at the owner’s favorite weekend track events.\nThe premium Bavarian automaker from Germany, BMW , is currently in the midst of morphing all of its models according to the Neue Klasse lifestyle, which includes a new design outside and inside plus a dedicated platform for the EV models. But not everyone is happy with the styling, as always, given some of the controversial traits, like the BMW Panoramic Vision or the kidney grille treatment, among others.\nNo worries, not even the current models have received unanimous praise, as many of them, the BMW M4 included, have received contentious traits, like the humongous air intakes of the G82/83 iteration. Well, on this occasion, you’re safe, as this used 2015 BMW M4 is of the tamer F82 variety, and it’s also intended for track use, meaning you can escape discussions in a safe and performance-oriented manner by saying that you’re focused on the weekend track days rather than (BMW’s) current politics.\nThe high-performance coupe version of the BMW 4 Series, the BMW M4 in the F82 iteration , has the S55 twin-turbocharged inline-six 3.0L engine under the hood, and this particular example is said to churn out 425 horsepower and 406 lb-ft of torque as per the factory rating. It’s also paired with the quick-shifting 7-speed dual-clutch transmission, and the white and black example has 44,418 miles on the odometer.\nAlas, this Alpine White over Black BMW M4 has given those miles a run for their money, as it has “been extensively modified with a clear focus on track performance”; the suspension has Eibach race springs, Ground Control camber/caster plates, and Hotchkis race anti-sway bars and control arms. The braking performance comes courtesy of AP Racing Competition and StopTech braided SST brake lines, while Apex SM-10 wheels provide a lightweight and motorsport-inspired setup for the “square tire” goodies.\nThe latter include 275-width Nitto NT01 tires on all wheels, and inside the BMW M4 comes with additional track-focused equipment: a StudioRSR Tesseract roll cage, OMP WRC racing seats, and Sabelt 5-point racing harnesses. The carbon-fiber roof reduces weight and lowers the center of gravity, and the whole ride now sits closer to the ground thanks to all the other enhancements.\n“This augmented F82 is ready to get back on the track with a new owner behind the wheel,” says the description from the dealership, but not before we also hear about the S55 powertrain’s performance-oriented upgrades: a Turner X-pipe with a catalytic converter delete and a Turner high-kick carbon-fiber rear spoiler.\n“With its low 44,418-mile showing, Alpine White exterior, carbon-fiber roof, 7-speed DCT, and extensive collection of track-focused upgrades, this 2015 BMW M4 offers a compelling combination of modern BMW performance and motorsport-inspired hardware,” says the conclusion. But are you ready for the pricing details? Well, it turns out that this modded BMW M4 is offered at $46,900, which isn’t bad for the bundle of upgrades.\nSo, what do you think – yay or nay? After all, a brand-new BMW M4 Coupe starts from at least at $82,200 sans destination and potential dealer hikes. Even the entry-level BMW M2 is now $69,500, while the M3 sedan is only slightly cheaper than an M4 starting at over $79k.\nView this post on Instagram A post shared by Garage Kept Motors LLC (@garagekeptmotors)\nVia // Garage Kept Motors\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nF82 BMW M4 BMW M4 BMW m4 Coupe track-ready for sale Garage Kept Motors\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Young Man Builds Insane Chevy C10 Truck in His Driveway: It Packs V8 Punch and 600 Horsepower",
